---
import ProjectDetail from '../../layouts/ProjectDetail.astro';
---

<ProjectDetail
  title="SEO Writer"
  description="A multi-user content platform for researching, outlining and writing SEO articles and landing pages in a brand's voice."
  tags={['FastAPI', 'Python', 'SQLAlchemy', 'Alembic', 'MariaDB', 'React', 'TypeScript', 'Vite', 'TanStack Query', 'OpenAI', 'Anthropic Claude', 'Google Search Console API']}
  liveDemo="https://mariam.renode.space/"
  liveDemoLabel="Live app"
  demoImages={[
    { src: '/screenshots/seo-writer-1.webp', alt: 'SEO Writer research step: target keyword, location and language fields, an optional existing article to update, and a list of ranking sources to select from.' },
    { src: '/screenshots/seo-writer-2.webp', alt: 'SEO Writer research results: search intent, content gaps, related terms to cover, and tabs for questions to answer, AI visibility tips and an entity matrix.' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="project-grid">
    <article class="project-card summary-card">
      <h2>Overview</h2>
      <p>
        SEO Writer started as a Streamlit prototype. Streamlit's rerun model caused UI bugs and its free tier hit memory limits on long research runs, so I rebuilt it as a real application: a layered FastAPI backend with a JSON API and a Vite/React/TypeScript frontend. It is now deployed and used with accounts, private brands and monthly usage limits.
      </p>
      <div class="summary-list">
        <div>
          <strong>Status</strong>
          <p>Live and in active development.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="project-card detail-card">
      <h2>What it does</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Article Writer:</strong> research the SERP, extract evidence from competitor pages, build an outline or skeleton, plan the layout, draft section by section, and produce a final article. Supports several article types (guide, how-to, listicle, comparison, review, tutorial, why).</li>
        <li><strong>Landing Page Optimizer:</strong> scrapes a page, detects its sections and generates per-section copy variations. Competitors come from live search.</li>
        <li><strong>Creative mode</strong> for freer, voice-led writing.</li>
        <li><strong>Meta titles and descriptions</strong> generated from the SERP and the brand voice.</li>
        <li><strong>Quality check:</strong> drafts are checked against a written editorial rule set plus required keywords and banned words, with automatic revision passes.</li>
        <li><strong>Search Console analytics dashboard</strong> per brand, with a hand-off from a finding into the writer.</li>
        <li><strong>Word (.docx) and Markdown export,</strong> and a live progress stream while long steps run.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div class="section-content">
      <h2>Architecture</h2>

      <div class="section-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Backend</h3>
          <p>
            FastAPI, SQLAlchemy and Alembic migrations, MariaDB in production (SQLite for tests). Layered as api / services / domain / infrastructure, with pure domain rules (roles, access policies, quotas, workflow state) that are unit tested.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Frontend</h3>
          <p>
            React 18, TypeScript, Vite, TanStack Query, React Router, Vitest. Served by a small Node server that proxies <code>/api</code> so login cookies work on one origin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Model routing</h3>
          <p>
            Models are routed by job: GPT-4o mini for structured extraction and JSON tasks, Claude Sonnet for sustained prose and voice. Every provider call goes through a metered client, so usage is tracked per user.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Long-running steps</h3>
          <p>
            Long steps (60 to 300 seconds) run as background jobs so proxies never cut the connection. A second identical request joins the running one, so a double click cannot pay for the same work twice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Workflow state</h3>
          <p>
            Workflow state is saved per run in the database, with a per-run lock so concurrent requests cannot overwrite each other.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div class="section-content">
      <h2>Security and access</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Accounts with Argon2 password hashing, cookie sessions and CSRF protection, plus login rate limiting and invite links.</li>
        <li>Roles (owner, admin, member, analytics-only) and per-brand access (owner, editor, viewer) so brand knowledge stays private to the people it is shared with.</li>
        <li>SSRF-guarded scraper.</li>
        <li>Optional bring-your-own API keys, and encrypted per-brand Google keys for Search Console.</li>
        <li>Monthly plan limits (Trial, Unlimited) on searches and model tokens, from an append-only usage ledger.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div class="section-content">
      <h2>Testing and quality</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>27 backend test files (API flow, access control, quotas, prompt snapshots, scraper, SSRF guard, analytics), frontend component tests, ESLint, Prettier and type checking.</li>
        <li>Offline test mode with canned model and search responses.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section project-usage">
    <h2>Current limitation</h2>
    <p>
      The app runs as a single worker on purpose. Run locks, the live progress stream, background jobs and the login rate limiter are in-process. Scaling beyond one worker means moving those to Redis. That is the main thing between this and a larger deployment.
    </p>
  </section>
</ProjectDetail>
